# Spacio Client Rules (Angular 22 & Three.js)

## 1. Directory Structure
Enforce strict separation within `src/app/`:
- `core/`: Singleton services (WebSocket client, camera orchestration, authentication, global app state).
- `shared/`: Reusable UI controls, 3D math utilities, shared interfaces, and standalone pipes/directives.
- `features/`: Isolated domain modules (e.g., `viewport`, `blueprint-editor`, `room-walkthrough`).

## 2. Angular 22 Coding Standards
- **Standalone Only:** All components, directives, and pipes must be standalone (`standalone: true`). Never introduce `NgModules`.
- **Reactivity & Signals:** Manage component local and reactive state using Angular Signals (`signal()`, `computed()`, `input()`, `output()`). Use RxJS exclusively for HTTP streams and WebSocket event pipelines.
- **Dependency Injection:** Use the modern `inject(ServiceClass)` syntax instead of constructor parameter injection.
- **Control Flow:** Use modern `@if`, `@else`, `@for`, and `@switch` template syntax. Never use `*ngIf` or `*ngFor`.
- **Template & Style Separation:** Templates MUST use external `.html` files via `templateUrl`. Styles MUST use external `.scss` files via `styleUrl`. Never use inline `template` or `styles` arrays in `@Component` decorators.

## 3. Three.js & WebGL Rules
- **DOM & Lifecycle Safety:** Restrict canvas creation, WebGL context acquisition, and Three.js scene initializations to `afterNextRender` lifecycle hooks to keep client-side rendering clean.
- **Render Loop Optimization:** Run `requestAnimationFrame` render loops outside the Angular detection zone using `NgZone.runOutsideAngular()` to guarantee an uninhibited 60 FPS.